@inherits BaseComponent

<div class="help-article-body">
    @switch ($"{Module.Slug}/{Topic.Slug}")
    {
        case "settings/general":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What this page is for</h2>
            <p>General settings hold your <strong>account profile</strong> — the name shown in the app, optional mobile number, and sign-in password. This is separate from mailbox credentials under Workspace.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Fields on the page</h2>
            <dl class="help-article-dl">
                <div>
                    <dt>First name / Last name</dt>
                    <dd>Required. Each must be 2–50 characters. The header avatar and display name update from these fields as you type.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Email</dt>
                    <dd>Your sign-in address. Read-only on this page — it cannot be changed here.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Mobile</dt>
                    <dd>Optional contact number. Up to 20 characters.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Password</dt>
                    <dd>Your app sign-in password, stored encrypted. Leave blank on save to keep the current password. When you enter a new value, it must be 6–255 characters.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Saving</h2>
            <p>Click <strong>Save</strong> after editing. Validation runs before the request is sent — fix any field errors shown under the inputs. A success pill confirms when the profile is stored.</p>
            break;

        case "settings/email-providers":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What this page is for</h2>
            <p>Email providers are <strong>IMAP/SMTP templates</strong> — server presets the app uses when you connect a mailbox. They do not store your mailbox password; credentials live on each connected account under Workspace.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">System vs custom templates</h2>
            <ul class="help-article-list">
                <li><strong>System</strong> templates (Gmail, Outlook, etc.) are read-only. Confirm hosts and ports match your provider, or ask your admin to update the seed data.</li>
                <li><strong>Custom</strong> templates are yours to add, edit, or delete when you use a host that is not in the catalog.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Fields on the page</h2>
            <dl class="help-article-dl">
                <div>
                    <dt>Name</dt>
                    <dd>Label shown when picking a provider for a mailbox.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>IMAP / SMTP</dt>
                    <dd>Incoming and outgoing server host, port, and SSL. Required for every active template.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Preset providers</h2>
            <p class="help-article-lead">When connecting an account, choose the matching preset. You usually need an <strong>app password</strong> or IMAP enabled — not your normal web-login password.</p>

            <section class="help-provider-card">
                <h3 class="help-provider-title">Gmail</h3>
                <p>Enable IMAP in Google Account settings. Create an app password under Security → 2-Step Verification → App passwords. Use that app password when saving the mailbox.</p>
                <p><a class="settings-link" href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/185833" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google: Sign in with app passwords</a></p>
            </section>

            <section class="help-provider-card">
                <h3 class="help-provider-title">Outlook.com / Microsoft 365</h3>
                <p>Ensure IMAP is enabled for the mailbox. Microsoft may require an app password or modern auth depending on your tenant.</p>
                <p><a class="settings-link" href="https://support.microsoft.com/office/outlook" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft Outlook help</a></p>
            </section>

            <section class="help-provider-card">
                <h3 class="help-provider-title">Yahoo Mail</h3>
                <p>Generate an app password in Yahoo Account Security and use it as the mailbox password.</p>
                <p><a class="settings-link" href="https://help.yahoo.com/kb/SLN4075.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Yahoo: Generate app password</a></p>
            </section>

            <section class="help-provider-card">
                <h3 class="help-provider-title">iCloud Mail</h3>
                <p>Enable two-factor authentication, then create an app-specific password for mail.</p>
                <p><a class="settings-link" href="https://support.apple.com/icloud" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple iCloud support</a></p>
            </section>

            <section class="help-provider-card">
                <h3 class="help-provider-title">Zoho Mail</h3>
                <p>Enable IMAP in Zoho Mail settings and use your mailbox password or an application-specific password if required.</p>
                <p><a class="settings-link" href="https://www.zoho.com/mail/help/imap-access.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zoho: IMAP access</a></p>
            </section>

            <section class="help-provider-card">
                <h3 class="help-provider-title">Fastmail</h3>
                <p>Use Fastmail’s documented IMAP/SMTP hostnames. App passwords are available under Password &amp; Security.</p>
                <p><a class="settings-link" href="https://www.fastmail.com/help/technical/servernames.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fastmail: Server names</a></p>
            </section>

            <section class="help-provider-card">
                <h3 class="help-provider-title">Custom provider</h3>
                <p>Add a custom template with your host’s IMAP and SMTP settings. Ask your IT team or host documentation if you are unsure of the server names or ports.</p>
            </section>
            break;

        case "settings/appearance":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What this page is for</h2>
            <p>Appearance controls the <strong>color theme</strong> on this browser — dark or light. Changes apply immediately; there is no separate save button.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Theme</h2>
            <p>Use the segmented <strong>Dark</strong> / <strong>Light</strong> control. The same choice is available from the sun/moon toggle in the top header — both stay in sync on this device.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What is not synced</h2>
            <p>Theme is stored locally in this browser only. It does not follow your account to other browsers, devices, or machines. Each place you sign in remembers its own theme.</p>
            break;

        case "workspace/contacts":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What this page is for</h2>
            <p>Contacts are <strong>use-case references</strong> — people your workflows and agents should recognize by name. This is not a full address book; add only the people you expect rules or chat to mention.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Fields on the page</h2>
            <dl class="help-article-dl">
                <div>
                    <dt>First name</dt>
                    <dd>Required. Shown as the list title. Up to 50 characters.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Last name</dt>
                    <dd>Optional. Combined with first name for display. Up to 50 characters.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Email</dt>
                    <dd>Optional. Normalized to lowercase on save. Must be unique among your contacts when set.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Phone</dt>
                    <dd>Optional. Up to 32 characters.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Alias</dt>
                    <dd>Short handle for agents (for example <code>jane</code> → <code>contact:jane</code>). Optional in the form — if left blank on create, the app generates one from the name. Must be unique among your contacts. On edit, changing the display name does not change an existing alias unless you clear and re-save it.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Context</dt>
                    <dd>Optional facts for agents — relationship, preferences, or how this person should be treated in triage and rules.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">List actions</h2>
            <p>Use <strong>Edit</strong> to change a contact or <strong>Delete</strong> to remove it permanently (soft delete internally). The list shows each contact by name, with entity ref, email, and phone on the second line when present.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Tags and buckets</h2>
            <p>Contacts can be linked to tags and buckets for organization and agent context. Assignment pickers on this page are not shown yet — that linking flow comes in a later phase. The underlying data model is ready.</p>
            break;

        case "workspace/email-accounts":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What this page is for</h2>
            <p>Email accounts are your <strong>connected mailboxes</strong>. Each account stores the address, login credentials, and agent reference for one inbox. IMAP/SMTP server settings come from a provider preset under Settings — not from this page.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Before you start</h2>
            <p>Pick or add an IMAP/SMTP template under <NavLink href="/help/settings/email-providers">Settings → Email providers</NavLink> first. When saving an account here, choose the matching provider preset. See that help article for Gmail app passwords, Outlook IMAP, and other host-specific setup.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Fields on the page</h2>
            <dl class="help-article-dl">
                <div>
                    <dt>Provider</dt>
                    <dd>IMAP/SMTP template for this mailbox. Changing it updates which servers the app uses on the next connection test or mail operation.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Email</dt>
                    <dd>The mailbox address you receive mail at. Must be unique among your connected accounts.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Username</dt>
                    <dd>IMAP/SMTP login. Often the same as the email address, but some hosts use a different username.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Password</dt>
                    <dd>Mailbox or app password, stored encrypted. Required when adding an account. When editing, leave blank to keep the saved password.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Default</dt>
                    <dd>Marks which account the Email agent uses when no specific mailbox is named. Set from the list with <strong>Make default</strong> — not in the editor. Your first account is set as default automatically on create.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Alias</dt>
                    <dd>Short handle for agents and settings (for example <code>work</code> → <code>mailbox:work</code>). Optional in the form — if left blank, the app generates one from your email address. Must be unique among your accounts.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Context</dt>
                    <dd>Optional facts for agents — how this mailbox is used, who it belongs to, or triage preferences.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Buckets / Tags</dt>
                    <dd>Optional organization shared with contacts and other workspace records. Assignment pickers on this page are not shown yet — linking mailboxes to tags and buckets comes in a later phase.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Test connection</h2>
            <p>After entering credentials, use <strong>Test connection</strong> to verify IMAP and SMTP before saving. The test uses what is in the form — including a new password you typed, or the stored password when the field is left blank on edit.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Multiple accounts</h2>
            <p>You can connect more than one inbox. The list shows each account by its alias; the default account drives the Email agent today. Use <strong>Make default</strong> to switch which inbox the agent uses when none is specified.</p>
            break;

        case "workspace/buckets":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What buckets are for</h2>
            <p>Buckets group contacts and mailboxes under a shared label — for example a client, project, or household. Agents and rules can treat everything in a bucket as related context.</p>
            <p>Buckets are optional. You can create them here first, or link them later when editing contacts and mailboxes.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Fields on the page</h2>
            <dl class="help-article-dl">
                <div>
                    <dt>Name</dt>
                    <dd>Display label in lists and pickers (for example <strong>XYZ Inc</strong> or <strong>Family</strong>). Not required to be unique.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Color</dt>
                    <dd>Optional swatch in the UI. Pick a preset or enter a hex color such as <code>#6366f1</code>.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Alias</dt>
                    <dd>Short handle for agents (for example <code>xyz</code> → <code>bucket:xyz</code>). Optional in the form — if left blank, the app generates one from the name on save. Must be unique among your buckets.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Context</dt>
                    <dd>Optional facts for agents — what this group represents, how to treat its members, or triage notes.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Pause vs delete</h2>
            <p><strong>Pause</strong> hides a bucket from pickers and runtime use but keeps the row. You can activate it again later. Existing links on contacts and mailboxes stay in the database but inactive buckets no longer appear when loading or assigning taxonomy.</p>
            <p><strong>Delete</strong> removes the bucket from your workspace permanently (soft delete internally) and unlinks it from all contacts and mailboxes.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Linking members</h2>
            <p>When editing a contact or mailbox, you can assign buckets to that record. A record can belong to multiple buckets. Bucket assignment pickers on those pages may be hidden until that flow is wired in the UI — the underlying data model is ready.</p>
            break;

        case "workspace/tags":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What tags are for</h2>
            <p>Tags are lightweight labels you attach to contacts and mailboxes — roles, topics, priorities, or facets such as <strong>VIP</strong> or <strong>Newsletter</strong>. They help filter and give agents extra context without grouping records into a single bucket.</p>
            <p>Tags are optional. Create them here, then link them when editing contacts and mailboxes.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Fields on the page</h2>
            <dl class="help-article-dl">
                <div>
                    <dt>Name</dt>
                    <dd>Display label in lists and pickers. Not required to be unique — the alias is the stable handle.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Color</dt>
                    <dd>Optional swatch in the UI. Pick a preset or enter a hex color such as <code>#22c55e</code>.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Alias</dt>
                    <dd>Short handle for agents (for example <code>vip</code> → <code>tag:vip</code>). Optional in the form — if left blank, the app generates one from the name on save. Must be unique among your tags.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Context</dt>
                    <dd>Optional facts for agents — when to apply this tag, who qualifies, or how to treat tagged records.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Pause vs delete</h2>
            <p><strong>Pause</strong> hides a tag from pickers and runtime use but keeps the row. You can activate it again later. Existing links on contacts and mailboxes stay in the database but inactive tags no longer appear when loading or assigning taxonomy.</p>
            <p><strong>Delete</strong> removes the tag from your workspace permanently (soft delete internally) and unlinks it from all contacts and mailboxes.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Tags vs buckets</h2>
            <p>Use <strong>tags</strong> for cross-cutting labels (many tags per record). Use <strong>buckets</strong> for grouping records under a shared umbrella (client, project, household). A contact or mailbox can have both.</p>
            break;

        case "chat/email-agent":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What chat is for</h2>
            <p>Chat is where you talk to agents in a thread. Each conversation keeps its own history and agent choice until you start a new thread or delete one.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Before you start</h2>
            <p>Connect at least one mailbox under <NavLink href="/help/workspace/email-accounts">Workspace → Email accounts</NavLink> and set a <strong>default</strong> account. The Email agent reads from that default inbox when no specific mailbox is named.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Opening chat</h2>
            <p>Chat lives in the sidebar — not on a separate settings page. Click <strong>New chat</strong> to create a thread (Email agent by default) and open it. Use <strong>Recent</strong> in the sidebar to switch back to an earlier conversation.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Choosing an agent</h2>
            <p>At the bottom of the composer, open the agent picker:</p>
            <ul class="help-article-list">
                <li><strong>Assistant</strong> — general help and questions that do not need mailbox access.</li>
                <li><strong>Email</strong> — triage and actions on your connected inboxes. Requires a default mailbox.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The choice is saved per thread. New chats start on <strong>Email</strong> so inbox shortcuts show immediately. Switch to Assistant from the picker when you do not need mail. If Email is unavailable, the picker shows an error with a link to Workspace → Email accounts.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Sending messages</h2>
            <p>Type in the composer and press <strong>Enter</strong> to send. Use <strong>Shift+Enter</strong> for a new line. While the assistant is replying, sending is disabled and a typing indicator appears.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">First triage run</h2>
            <p>Follow <NavLink href="/help/getting-started">Getting started</NavLink> for the ordered setup path. On the chat step, open a new thread and use a shortcut or send a simple triage request to confirm end-to-end mail access.</p>
            break;

        case "chat/threads":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What threads are</h2>
            <p>Each chat thread is a separate conversation with its own title, message history, and agent choice. Threads appear under <strong>Recent</strong> in the sidebar.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Starting a thread</h2>
            <p>Click <strong>New chat</strong> in the sidebar. A thread is created with the title “New chat”, the Email agent, and inbox shortcuts on the empty state. Rename it once you know what the conversation is about. Connect a mailbox first or you will be sent to Workspace → Email accounts.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Renaming</h2>
            <p>Hover a thread in Recent and click the rename icon. Edit the title inline, then press <strong>Enter</strong> or click away to save. Press <strong>Escape</strong> to cancel. Titles can be up to 80 characters.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Switching threads</h2>
            <p>Click any thread in Recent to open it. The main area loads that thread’s messages and restores its last selected agent.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Deleting</h2>
            <p>Delete a thread from the sidebar when you no longer need it. If you delete the last thread, the app creates a fresh one so you always have somewhere to chat.</p>
            break;

        case "workflows/overview":
            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What workflows will do</h2>
            <p>Workflows will run <strong>automations</strong> on mail and other events — scheduled triage, repeat actions, and rules you define once instead of asking chat each time. The module is not built yet; the page shows a placeholder until it ships.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">Before workflows ship</h2>
            <p>Complete the setup path first:</p>
            <ul class="help-article-list">
                <li><NavLink href="/help/settings/general">Settings → General</NavLink> — profile and sign-in</li>
                <li><NavLink href="/help/settings/email-providers">Settings → Email providers</NavLink> — IMAP/SMTP presets</li>
                <li><NavLink href="/help/workspace/email-accounts">Workspace → Email accounts</NavLink> — connected inboxes</li>
                <li><NavLink href="/help/chat/email-agent">Chat → Email agent</NavLink> — confirm triage works</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Optional: add <NavLink href="/help/workspace/contacts">contacts</NavLink>, <NavLink href="/help/workspace/tags">tags</NavLink>, and <NavLink href="/help/workspace/buckets">buckets</NavLink> so future rules have rich workspace context.</p>

            <h2 class="help-article-heading">What to expect later</h2>
            <p>When workflows arrive, you will define triggers (for example new mail, time of day) and actions (triage, tag, notify). Chat will help draft workflow definitions from natural language. Until then, use chat for one-off triage and keep workspace data organized.</p>
            break;

        default:
            <p class="settings-hint">Help for this topic is coming soon.</p>
            break;
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required HelpModule Module { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required HelpTopic Topic { get; set; }
}
